 # Architecture
 
-```mermaid
-flowchart LR
-  Dashboard -->|REST /ai-soar| Service[AI/SOAR :8004]
-  Service -->|GET /v1 REST only| Core[SIEM Backend :8003]
-  Service --> DB[(sentinelzone_ai_soar)]
-  Service --> Redactor --> Provider[Mock / OpenAI / Local]
-  Provider --> Validator[Schema + citation validation]
-  Validator --> Ledger
-  Analyst --> Proposal --> Operator[Independent approval]
-  Operator --> Policy[Current target validation]
-  Policy --> Claim[Durable execution claim]
-  Claim --> DryRun[Dry-run executor]
-  DryRun --> Verify[Separate state query]
-  Verify --> Audit
-  Audit --> Rollback[Rollback + removal verification]
-```
+Dashboard `:3001` has independent server-side routes to Core `:8003` and AI/SOAR `:8004`. AI/SOAR reads Core only via authenticated GET REST and owns a separate PostgreSQL DB/role `sentinelzone_ai_soar`. It imports no Core source/models/ORM. No frontend source is bundled.
 
-No core database model, ORM session, dashboard module, telemetry connector, or source file is imported. `CoreBackendClient` is the only source of incident evidence and inventory. It has fixture and HTTP implementations. AI provider and executor abstractions are independent.
-
-## Phase 28
-
-Context selection preserves source diversity, prefers severity/recent events, is deterministic, limits event count, and enforces a character budget. Incident metadata, selected event summaries, timeline, asset context, correlation reasons and missing telemetry are sent. The legacy core cannot supply identity context or sensor coverage; the adapter says UNKNOWN explicitly.
-
-Redaction runs before truncation and before provider access. The stored snapshot is also redacted. Each provider receives the same trusted system instruction and a separate JSON object labeled UNTRUSTED EVENT DATA. There are no model tools, actions or executable output. The mock never interprets log instructions.
-
-All five AI output fields are mandatory. Extra keys, malformed JSON, duplicate JSON keys, uncited facts, excessive lists, refusal, truncation and unavailable dependencies fail safely. Citations must exist remotely, belong to the same incident and bound tenant, and be in the selected context. Invalid output is quarantined in ai_analyses with trusted=false; normal API responses expose no invalid analysis body.
-
-The ledger stores context, output status and stage metadata, not hidden model reasoning. Stage duration is measured locally. Retrieval gives the redacted snapshot and ordered stages, allowing offline reproduction with the deterministic mock. Runs are synchronous bounded requests; this release does not provide a distributed job queue. Interrupted runs remain visibly incomplete and are not silently replayed.
-
-## Phase 29
-
-Roles are deliberately separate: analyst proposes, operator approves/executes/rolls back, admin inspects and manages deployment policy. Admin is not an implicit operator. Both approval and execution verify expiry; requester and approver must differ, even after a role change. An inactive or no-longer-operator approver invalidates execution authorization.
-
-Protected names, asset aliases, inventory addresses, normalized roles and configured CIDRs are checked before proposal, approval and execution. Inventory pagination failures deny action. BLOCK_IP accepts only canonical literal IPs. SUSPEND_TEST_PROCESS accepts only registered assets and a `test-` process name with lab_only=true. Parameters cannot contain shell commands or executor URLs.
-
-YAML is parsed with safe_load and validated as an acyclic dependency graph. Mandatory validation, approval, execution, verification and rollback stages are connected in order. Only allowlisted actions and declarative equality conditions are supported. No Python, shell or template expression evaluation exists. Rollback is available, never automatically run after successful execution.
-
-Database compare-and-set transitions and a unique `(proposal_id, operation)` claim coordinate workers. Claims commit before side effects. A replay or restart cannot invoke the same proposal again. This provides at-most-once dispatch, not a claim of exactly-once network side effects. A failed/uncertain execution needs explicit investigation or rollback. Database failures after dispatch leave a durable uncertain claim.
-
-## Tables
+| Module | Responsibility |
+| --- | --- |
+| app/connectors/core_backend.py | read-only REST, scoped resource IDs, bounded complete pagination, citation lookups |
+| app/connectors/mapping.py | explicit old/normalized response mapping, optional telemetry allowlist, tenant enforcement |
+| app/ai/context | deterministic evidence selection, credential redaction before truncation, bounded data envelope |
+| app/ai/providers | mock/OpenAI/local interfaces; static system instruction, no tools/action authority |
+| app/ai/validation | strict five-section schema and current existence/incident/tenant/selection checks |
+| app/ai/investigation | persisted runs/steps/analyses; redacted snapshots, no raw model exception or hidden reasoning |
+| app/security | independent hashed service tokens, explicit roles, request size cap |
+| app/soar/proposals, approvals | immutable proposal, fresh protection, independent approval, expiry, executor binding |
+| app/soar/executors, verification, rollback | durable at-most-once claim, separate verification, fail-closed real seams, simulator restoration |
+| app/api/responses.py | stable dashboard schemas, tri-state verification, structured errors |
+| migrations | versioned dedicated schema, including preserved v0.29.0 claims |
+
+## Persistence
 
 | Table | Purpose |
 | --- | --- |
-| ai_runs | Tenant, incident, provider/model, status, timestamps, redacted context |
-| ai_steps | Ordered stage ledger, citation IDs, durations and references |
-| ai_analyses | Structured output, validation result, trust marker |
-| action_proposals | Immutable target/parameters/playbook snapshot, authorization state |
-| action_approvals | One independent human decision per proposal |
-| action_executions | Unique execution/rollback claim and verified result |
-| action_audit | Proposal decisions, refusals, claims, results and reconciliation |
-| service_users | Separate tenant-scoped identities and token hashes |
-| simulated_effects | Durable simulator target state, never a live system state |
+| ai_runs | tenant/incident, provider/model, status and redacted context |
+| ai_steps | ordered stage ledger with durations and evidence references |
+| ai_analyses | structured output, trust flag and validation result |
+| action_proposals | immutable target, parameters, playbook snapshot, pinned executor, authorization status |
+| action_approvals | single independent decision |
+| action_executions | unique durable execute/rollback claims, outcomes and verification |
+| action_audit | proposals, decisions, denials, claims, results and reconciliation |
+| service_users | separate tenant users, roles, active state, random-token SHA-256 hashes |
+| simulated_effects | durable simulator state only |
+
+Production requires the dedicated PostgreSQL database and role, and real Core mode. Development/test can use SQLite and explicit fixtures. The single lab has a temporary opt-in tenant-less Core binding; strict tenant-aware responses remain supported. Optional CryptoGuard context does not change transport permissions.
+
+At-most-once dispatch depends on preservation of the dedicated DB's committed claims. A crash after claim but before/after a remote effect leaves an uncertain state; no automatic redispatch occurs. Verification queries state, never trusts a dispatch exit code. A new proposal is a distinct authorized operation; cross-proposal target conflicts require ownership-aware logic in a future live adapter.
 
-Application APIs do not modify or delete audit records. They are not a cryptographically tamper-evident ledger against a database administrator. Apply database access control and backup policy accordingly.
+The service has no job queue or automatic AI resume. Analysis is synchronous and may remain running after worker interruption. Provider unavailability degrades health while the dedicated DB-backed APIs remain available. Audit is application-append-only and not tamper-proof against a database owner.
