+# Dashboard integration — no frontend source changes included
+
+The existing Next.js dashboard at `C:\SOC-Main\frontend` (`:3001`) remains owned by the other integration workstream. This release supplies a stable API contract and the following server-side proxy design. It does not redesign or modify the dashboard.
+
+## Route mapping
+
+Use a separate Next.js route namespace. The browser authenticates to your existing dashboard session; **only the Next.js server** obtains the specific user's AI/SOAR credential from an encrypted server-side secret store.
+
+| Frontend request | Next.js upstream request | AI/SOAR role |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `GET /api/ai-soar/health` | `GET http://127.0.0.1:8004/health` | authenticated dashboard session; upstream public health |
+| `POST /api/ai-soar/ai/analyze` | `POST /v1/ai/analyze` | analyst |
+| `GET /api/ai-soar/ai/runs/{run_id}` | `GET /v1/ai/runs/{run_id}` | any service user in tenant |
+| `GET /api/ai-soar/incidents/{incident_id}/analyses` | `GET /v1/incidents/{incident_id}/analyses` | any service user in tenant |
+| `POST /api/ai-soar/actions` | `POST /v1/actions` | analyst |
+| `GET /api/ai-soar/actions` | `GET /v1/actions` | any service user in tenant |
+| `GET /api/ai-soar/actions/{proposal_id}` | `GET /v1/actions/{proposal_id}` | any service user in tenant |
+| `POST /api/ai-soar/actions/{proposal_id}/approve` | `POST /v1/actions/{proposal_id}/approve` | operator, different from requester |
+| `POST /api/ai-soar/actions/{proposal_id}/reject` | `POST /v1/actions/{proposal_id}/reject` | operator |
+| `POST /api/ai-soar/actions/{proposal_id}/execute` | `POST /v1/actions/{proposal_id}/execute` | operator |
+| `POST /api/ai-soar/actions/{proposal_id}/rollback` | `POST /v1/actions/{proposal_id}/rollback` | operator |
+| `GET /api/ai-soar/playbooks` | `GET /v1/playbooks` | any service user in tenant |
+| `GET /api/ai-soar/playbooks/{playbook_id}` | `GET /v1/playbooks/{playbook_id}` | any service user in tenant |
+| `GET /api/ai-soar/policy` | `GET /v1/policy` | admin only |
+
+The upstream base address is fixed server configuration, never a browser-supplied URL. If Next.js runs in a container, use its actual private AI/SOAR address, with HTTPS whenever traffic crosses an untrusted link. Only `limit` and `offset` pagination query parameters may be forwarded to list endpoints. Allowlist methods and route patterns; do not implement an unrestricted URL proxy.
+
+## Server-side identity requirements
+
+- Reuse the dashboard's verified session middleware. Resolve a one-to-one mapping `(dashboard user ID, tenant) -> AI/SOAR user token` on the server. Do not trust user/role/tenant headers submitted by the browser.
+- The analyst and approver must resolve to **different service user IDs**. Never use a single shared service token for all dashboard users or let an analyst select an operator token. A principal has exactly one role; admin has no implicit operator rights.
+- Do not forward the browser's `Authorization` header. Set the upstream Bearer header from the resolved server-side secret only. `CORE_API_TOKEN` belongs only to AI/SOAR -> Core and must not be stored as a dashboard user credential.
+- Environment names such as `AI_SOAR_BASE_URL` are server-only. No service token/secret may use `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`, client props, HTML, source maps or localStorage.
+- Require same-origin/CSRF protection on all POST routes in addition to session auth. Upstream Bearer auth does not secure a cookie-authenticated Next.js proxy against CSRF.
+- Use `cache: no-store`, `redirect: error`, bounded request/response sizes and a bounded timeout. Never log Authorization, raw request bodies or provider context.
+- Do not automatically retry POST calls. A timeout after execute may leave `EXECUTING` or `UNKNOWN`; fetch the proposal to inspect its durable state.
+
+There is deliberately no guessed auth implementation or deployable catch-all proxy here: the actual dashboard session/secret-store contract was not supplied. Implement the route mapping within the dashboard's existing server-side authentication framework. These requirements are the handoff contract for that workstream.
+
+## AI display contract
+
+`AIRunView` in `contracts/ai-run.schema.json` / `docs/openapi.json` supplies:
+
+`run_id`, `incident_id`, `status`, `trusted`, `validation_status`, `provider`, `model`, `created_at`, the five top-level analysis sections, `citations`, `data_mode`, `human_review_required`, plus compatibility `analysis`, validation details and the redacted step/context ledger.
+
+All five sections are **null** when the result is unavailable/invalid. Do not display an invalid model output as trusted analysis. `trusted=true` means schema/citations passed, not that the diagnosis is correct. Label mock provider output and TEST/REPLAY/UNKNOWN context prominently. Render all AI/event strings as plain escaped text; never use `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` or render model-supplied links/scripts as privileged UI.
+
+Link citations to your existing Core alert detail view using only `event_uid`. The service validates selected membership; the dashboard should not infer new citations from prose. Analyze returns 201 with a persisted run even if its `status` is `failed`/`rejected`; read the body, not just the HTTP success code. It is a synchronous route; a proxy timeout does not imply the server performed no work. Runs can be rediscovered by the incident analyses list.
+
+## SOAR display contract
+
+`ActionView` contains proposal identity, requested/approved user IDs, action and target, status, `approval_status`, detailed `approval`, expiry, `dry_run`, `effect_scope`, pinned `executor`, `execution_result`, `verification_result`, `rollback_available`, `rollback_result`, `restoration_verified`, `executions`, and `audit`.
+
+For `dry_run=true`, show **“Simulation — no firewall or endpoint changed.”** `SUCCESS` means simulated effect verified; `RESTORED` means simulated effect removed. Never use a green “IP blocked on pfSense” message for simulation. For future live adapters, `dry_run=false` alone is insufficient: show live containment as confirmed only when status is SUCCESS and `verification_result=true`.
+
+`verification_result`/`restoration_verified` are tri-state: true = verified, false = checked and not verified, null = not queried/unknown. `rollback_available` represents current eligibility; false before dispatch and after rollback. Audit contains the latest 200 entries in chronological order; `audit_truncated=true` means earlier rows remain in the DB and require an administrative export.
+
+Use human-clicked proposal/approve/execute controls. An AI containment suggestion may prefill a draft, but must not submit, approve or execute automatically. Disable self-approval in UI as a convenience; server enforcement is authoritative. On 409, refetch status rather than retry execution. `EXECUTING`, `UNKNOWN`, `ROLLING_BACK`, `ROLLBACK_UNKNOWN` are uncertain states; show an investigation/reconciliation message.
+
+No frontend build or end-to-end browser integration was performed in this release environment.
